//floranorthamerica.org/Special:Search?search=Maurandella+antirrhiniflora", "Wetland Plant List URL": "https://wetland-plants.sec.usace.army.mil/", "NRCS Web Soil Survey URL": "https://websoilsurvey.nrcs.usda.gov/app/", "EPA Ecoregions URL": "https://www.epa.gov/eco-research/ecoregions", "TPWD EMS URL": "https://tpwd.texas.gov/landwater/land/programs/landscape-ecology/ems/", "NPSOT Propagation Common Name": "Snapdragon Vine", "NPSOT Propagation Materials": "Seed | Stem", "NPSOT Propagation Instructions": "Seed — Treatment: No Treatment — Method: Sow them indoors in a seed tray filled with a well-draining seed starting mix. Keep the soil consistently moist and provide them with bright, indirect light. Once seedlings develop their first set of true leaves, they can be gently transplanted into individual pots. — Collection: Harvest seeds from mature seed pods after the flowers fade. || Stem — Treatment: Cutting — Method: The best time to propagate Maurandella antirrhiniflora is in the early spring or late summer, when the plant is actively growing. Remove the leaves from the bottom inch or two and dip the cut end in rooting hormone for an extra boost. Plant the cuttings in a good quality potting mix, keeping the soil consistently moist. Cover the pot with a plastic dome or humidity dome to help retain moisture and encourage root development. With consistent care, you’ll see new roots emerge within a few weeks. Provide your cuttings with bright, indirect light. Don’t let the soil dry out completely, but avoid overwatering. The soil should be consistently moist, but not soggy. — Collection: Snip a healthy stem section from your existing Maurandella antirrhiniflora plant. Choose non-flowering stems, ideally about 4-6 inches long.", "NPSOT Propagation Source URL": "https://www.npsot.org/posts/native-plant/maurandella-antirrhiniflora/", "NPSOT Propagation References": "https://propagate.one/how-to-propagate-maurandella-antirrhiniflora/?print=pdf"}, {"Scientific Name": "Melampodium leucanthum", "Common Name(s)": "Blackfoot Daisy, Rock Daisy, Plains Blackfoot, Arnica", "Duration": "Perennial", "Deciduous/ Evergreen (green in winter)": "Deciduous", "Growth Habitat": "Herb", "Sun/Shade Requirements": "Sun", "Water Req.": "Low", "Avg Height (ft)": "1–2", "Avg Width (ft)": "2–3", "Bloom Time": "Mar , Apr , May , Jun , Jul , Aug , Sep , Oct , Nov", "Unnamed: 25": "", "Size Notes": "Plants are seldom over 6 inches high, and the clump is 1-2 feet across. Occasionally found from 1-2 feet tall.", "Leaf": "", "Bloom Color": "White , Yellow", "Bloom Notes": "The yellow-orange disc flowers contrast sharply with the white ray flowers.", "USA": "AZ , CO , KS , NM , OK , TX", "Canada": "", "Native Distribution": "CO to OK, s. to AZ, c. TX & Mex.", "Native Habitat": "Throughout, Prairie, Plains, Meadows, Pastures, Savannas, Dry calcalreous substrates", "Water Use": "Low", "Light Requirement": "Sun , Part Shade", "Soil Moisture": "Dry", "Soil pH": "Alkaline (pH>7.2)", "CaCO3 Tolerance": "", "Drought Tolerance": "", "Soil Description": "Dry, rocky, calcareous soils. Rocky, Gravelly Sandy, Limestone-based, Caliche type", "Conditions Comments": "Blackfoot daisy is a sturdy, mounding plant, that will flourish in rock gardens. It is heat and drought tolerant. Good drainage is essential to its success. In late winter, older plants can be cut back halfway to keep them compact. Rich soil and abundant water will likely produce many more flowers in the short-term, but may consequently shorten the lifespan.", "Use Ornamental": "Showy, Rock gardens, Blooms ornamental, Long-blooming", "Use Wildlife": "Nectar-Bees, Nectar-Butterflies, Nectar-insects, Seeds-Granivorous birds", "Use Medicinal": "", "Use Other": "", "Interesting Foliage": "", "Propagation Material": "", "Description": "", "Seed Collection": "", "Commercially Avail": "", "Maintenance": "", "USDA": "Find Melampodium leucanthum in USDA Plants", "FNA": "Find Melampodium leucanthum in the Flora of North Amer